        {
            "text": "Swift has shorthand operators that combine one operator with an assignment, so you can change a variable in place. These look like the existing operators you know – `+`, `-`, `*`, and `/`, but they have an `=` on the end because they assign the result back to whatever variable you were using."
        },
        {
            "text": "For example, if someone scored 95 in an exam but needs to be penalized 5 points, you could write this:"
        },
        {
            "code": "var score = 95\nscore -= 5"
        },
        {
            "text": "Similarly, you can add one string to another using `+=`:"
        },
        {
            "code": "var quote = \"The rain in Spain falls mainly on the \"\nquote += \"Spaniards\""
        }
    ],
    "title": "Compound assignment operators",
